--   The `inf.prob`, `inf.prob.g2`, `act.rate`, `rec.rate` and `rec.rate.g2` arguments of `param.net()` no longer read a plain vector as varying with the duration of infection. Give such parameters with the new `by_infection_duration()`, either one value per time step since infection or by stage with `durations`: `inf.prob = by_infection_duration(c(0.5, 0.5, 0.1))` is the model that `inf.prob = c(0.5, 0.5, 0.1)` gave before, and `by_infection_duration(c(acute = 0.5, chronic = 0.1), durations = c(2, Inf))` writes the same parameter by stage, the last stage lasting until the infection ends. With the built-in model types, a plain vector of length greater than one is now an error, raised by `netsim()` before the simulation starts or by the built-in module that an extension model uses. Custom modules, which give plain vectors their own meaning, are unaffected. A `by_infection_duration()` object may be an entry of a `multilayer()` parameter. DCMs and ICMs refuse these objects, and `param.net_to_table()` stops on them (#1092).
+    Code that must run on both versions can pass `at` and `posit_ids` by name and the rest by position, as in `set_epi(dat, "i.num", value, at = at)`.
 
--   Removed the random parameter interface: the `random.params` argument of `param.net()` (including `param.random.set`), `param_random()`, `generate_random_params()` and `get_param_set()`. Passing `random.params` to `param.net()` is now an error, and so is running a `param.net` object that holds them (saved under 2.6.x, or set with `update_params()`). To vary parameters across simulations, draw the values in advance into a table with one row per draw and run each row as a scenario with `create_scenario_list()` and `use_scenario()`. The new Parameter Uncertainty section of the "Working with Model Parameters in EpiModel" vignette shows how, including correlated draws and a mapping from each part of the removed interface. To tabulate a single `param.net` object, use `param.net_to_table()`.
+-   With the built-in model types, `inf.prob`, `inf.prob.g2`, `act.rate`, `rec.rate` and `rec.rate.g2` that vary with the duration of infection are now given with `by_infection_duration()`, and a plain vector of length greater than one is an error. Replace `inf.prob = c(0.5, 0.5, 0.1)` with `by_infection_duration(c(0.5, 0.5, 0.1))`, or give it by stage with `by_infection_duration(c(acute = 0.5, chronic = 0.1), durations = c(2, Inf))`. Custom modules are unaffected (#1093).
 
--   `netsim()` no longer completes `param` with the parameters saved in `x` when restarting: `param` must hold all the parameters of the model, and `x` (like the `sim_obj` of `make_restart_point()`) no longer needs them. The internal `groups` parameter is restored from the `run` sublist. In extension models, a parameter that an initialization module sets with `set_param()` is lost on restart, as that module does not run again. For example, EpiModelHIV's `initialize_msm()` sets `time.unit` from `param$epistats`, so its `reinit_msm()` sets it again on restart.
+-   Removed the random parameter interface: `param.net(random.params = )`, `param_random()`, `generate_random_params()` and `get_param_set()`. To vary parameters across simulations, draw them into a table and run each row as a scenario, as shown in the Parameter Uncertainty section of the "Working with Model Parameters in EpiModel" vignette (#1086).
 
--   `netsim()` restarts every simulation from the first simulation of `x`, with a warning when `x` holds more than one (simulation `s` used to restart from simulation `(s - 1) %% n + 1` of the `n` in `x`). Select the simulation to restart from with `get_sims()` or `make_restart_point()`.
+-   On restart, `netsim()` no longer fills in `param` from the parameters saved in `x`, so `param` must hold every parameter of the model. Extension models whose initialization module sets a parameter with `set_param()` must set it again on restart, as EpiModelHIV's `reinit_msm()` does for `time.unit`.
 
--   `netsim()` now stops when the layers of a multi-layer model have different numbers of nodes, which 2.6.x accepted.
