 ### NEW FEATURES
 
--   Add `netclique()`, a clique network layer that `netsim()` accepts anywhere in its list of layers alongside `netest` fits. Every pair of nodes sharing a value of a grouping attribute is an edge, so each household, classroom, ward, or cabin is a clique, and the edges never form or dissolve. It is for structures that are groups by definition, with no tie formation process to estimate. The layer is skipped by the network resimulation and the edges correction, its edges are removed with departing nodes, and arriving nodes are placed on it by one of three rules: `"isolate"` (no edges and no group), `"new"` (a new group of size one, with an id never used before in the simulation), or `"join"` (an existing group, drawn in proportion to its size by default or chosen by a user function, with an edge to every current member, including other nodes joining in the same step). This replaces carrying a household layer through `param.net()` and a custom infection module, as in the EpiModel Gallery RSV example. `netdx()` refuses `netclique` objects, and `print()` summarizes the layer, including mean degree by a nodal attribute with `by`. `netclique()` stores the layer as a `networkLite`, which keeps even large layers small. `trim_netest()` accepts `netclique` and `netcensus` layers. Clique layers that share a grouping attribute cannot use the `"join"` rule, which would place an arrival in different groups on different layers. Closes #1081.
-
--   Add `sample_groups()` and `assign_groups()` to build the grouping attribute of a clique layer. `sample_groups()` builds a population of exactly the requested size from a table of group types with weights, such as household compositions by age group, returning each node's group id and the attributes its type implies. `assign_groups()` assigns group ids to an existing population: it draws group sizes from a target distribution, seeds groups with an anchor member (an adult), places dependent members (children) in the open slots of anchored groups, and fills the rest at random, so that every dependent shares a group with an anchor.
-
--   Add `move_to_group()`, which moves nodes to another group of a `netclique` layer during a simulation and rewires the layer to match, in either network storage mode: each moved node loses its edges to its old group and gains an edge to every active member of its new group. Every clique layer on the same grouping attribute is rewired, while setting the attribute alone rewires none. Moving nodes to an unused group id starts a new group, and `NA` takes a node out of its group. Nodes arriving in a time step can be moved only once `nwupdate.net()` has placed them by the layer's arrival rule.
+-   Add `netclique()`, a `netsim()` layer in which every pair of nodes sharing a grouping attribute (a household, classroom or cabin) is an edge that never forms or dissolves. Arriving nodes are left isolated, start a new group, or join an existing one. Closes #1081.
 
--   Add `netcensus()`, an observed network layer that `netsim()` accepts anywhere in its list of layers. It wraps a fully observed contact network, a static `network` or a `networkDynamic` with edge spells (without loops or parallel edges), and the simulation reads the edges active at each time step from it, in either network storage mode. Its vertex attributes become nodal attributes wherever the layer sits in the list. The node set is fixed, so `netsim()` refuses vital dynamics with a census layer, `arrive_nodes()` and `depart_nodes()` stop if a custom module tries to change it, and duration tracking is refused for a dynamic census, whose spells carry the durations. The observation window is half-open, `[start, end)`, like a `networkDynamic` spell. `netsim()` warns when the simulation ends before the window begins or `nsteps` reaches its end, past which only spells that extend beyond the window stay active. `print()` summarizes the edges active over the window. `make_restart_point()` refuses a dynamic census, which reads the observed network on the simulation clock; restart from the `netsim` object instead. This replaces the custom initialization and infection modules of the Network Modeling for Epidemics chapter on epidemics over observed networks. Closes #937.
+-   Add `sample_groups()` and `assign_groups()` to build the grouping attribute of a clique layer, and `move_to_group()` to move nodes between groups during a simulation.
 
--   `param.net()` now accepts `inf.prob`, `inf.prob.g2` and `act.rate` as `multilayer()` objects with one entry per network layer, and the built-in infection modules apply each layer's value to the discordant edges of that layer. Each entry may itself be a `by_infection_duration()` object. `crosscheck.net()` checks that a `multilayer` parameter covers every layer, and `param.net_to_table()` stops on one, since parameter tables cannot yet represent them (#1092).
+-   Add `netcensus()`, a `netsim()` layer that reads its edges at each time step from an observed static or dynamic network. Closes #937.
 
--   Add automatic attribute history tracking for network simulations. The attributes named in the new `tracked.attributes` argument of `control.net()` are recorded at each time step as delta-compressed changes: only the nodes whose values changed (including `NA` transitions), plus arriving and departing nodes. `active` is always tracked when tracking is on. The attributes named in `tracked.attributes.once`, such as `"race"` or `"birth.year"`, are recorded only in the initial snapshot and for arrivals. An attribute that a module creates on its first call is recorded for every node from the step it appears, and a tracked attribute missing at the end of a step is an error. On restart, the history starts again from a full snapshot at `start - 1`, as the history of `x` is not carried over.
+-   `param.net()` accepts `inf.prob`, `inf.prob.g2` and `act.rate` as `multilayer()` objects with one value per network layer. Parameter tables cannot yet represent `multilayer()` or `by_infection_duration()` values (#1092).
 
--   Add `get_attr_at()`, which reconstructs the tracked attributes of every active node at a past time step from the attribute history, as a `tibble` with one row per node. With `compute_age = TRUE`, it computes each node's age at that step from its last recorded `age`, which needs a `time.unit` parameter (days per time step).
+-   Add attribute history tracking with the `tracked.attributes` and `tracked.attributes.once` arguments of `control.net()`, and `get_attr_at()` to reconstruct the attributes of every active node at a past time step.
 
--   Add `make_networkDynamic()`, which reconstructs a `networkDynamic` object from a `netsim` simulation, with one edge per tie and layer, its spells from the cumulative edgelist, and vertex activity and attributes from the attribute history. This allows `ndtv` visualization and `tsna` temporal network analysis even of simulations run with `tergmLite = TRUE`. It needs every edge of the simulation, so it stops unless the simulation ran with `truncate.el.cuml = Inf`.
+-   Add `make_networkDynamic()`, which rebuilds a `networkDynamic` object from a `netsim` simulation, including one run with `tergmLite = TRUE`, for use with `ndtv` and `tsna`.
 
--   `merge.netsim()` gains `keep.run`, `keep.cumulative.edgelist`, `keep.attr.history` and `keep.raw.records`, which bind the per-simulation `run` sublists, cumulative edgelists and recorded histories; only `keep.cumulative.edgelist` defaults to `FALSE`. A merged object that keeps its `run` sublists can restart from any of its simulations, selected with `get_sims()`. Elements not kept are dropped from both `x` and `y`, which must then hold the same elements, per-simulation elements and network statistics. An element with its own `keep.*` argument (such as `run`) follows that argument even when listed in `save.other`.
+-   `merge.netsim()` gains `keep.run`, `keep.cumulative.edgelist`, `keep.attr.history` and `keep.raw.records`. A merged object that keeps `run` can restart from any of its simulations.
 
--   `control.dcm()` gains `ode.args`, a list of further arguments to `deSolve::ode()` such as `rtol` and `atol`, and `solver`, a function that replaces the call to `ode()` (for example, to restart the solver at abrupt changes in a model's rates). Extra columns a custom solver returns are kept as model outputs.
+-   `control.dcm()` gains `ode.args`, passed on to `deSolve::ode()`, and `solver`, a function that replaces the call to `ode()`.
 
 ### BUG FIXES
 
--   `print.dcm()` now prints list parameters and lists only the first 20 initial conditions and output variables of larger models, and `plot.dcm()` no longer starts the x-axis at 0 for models solved over times that start after 1, such as calendar years.
-
--   Fix `merge.netsim()` for three or more objects merged in sequence (`Reduce(merge, ...)`, or the batches of an HPC run) with `keep.network`, `keep.other` or `keep.diss.stats` set to `FALSE`, which failed with "x and y have different structure". Elements not kept may now be missing from one side; a kept element missing from one side is still an error, which now names the element and the `keep.*` argument that would drop it. The `save.other` elements are now bound like the other per-simulation elements, which keeps their `NULL` entries.
-
--   Fix `merge.netsim()` to bind the epi trackers of `y` by name rather than by position (trackers held in a different order were bound to the wrong tracker of `x`), to bind the per-simulation `coef.form` lists rather than keep those of `x` alone, and to keep the `transmat` class of `stats$transmat`, so that splitting an object with `get_sims()` and merging the parts back returns the original object.
-
--   Fix `merge.netsim()` to compare `set.control.tergm`, `set.control.ergm` and `dat.updates` with `all.equal()`, as it already did `monitors` and `nwstats.formula`, rather than `identical()`. Their environments differ between separate `control.net()` calls and once simulations are serialized and read back (returned by parallel workers, or saved as the batches of an HPC run), and the merge then failed with "x and y have different controls".
-
--   `merge.netsim()` no longer requires `x` and `y` to share the controls for how they were run rather than what they simulated: `ncores`, `verbose`, `verbose.int` and the checkpoint settings (`nsims` could already differ). Batches run with different `ncores` or checkpoint directories, or a batch of one simulation and a batch of several, failed with "x and y have different controls", since `netsim()` sets `ncores` to 1 for a single simulation and turns `verbose` off for several.
-
--   Fix `get_sims()`, and so `make_restart_point()`, to subset the per-simulation `coef.form`, `attr.history`, `raw.records` and `cumulative.edgelist` elements, which were returned for every simulation; a restart from a subset could then start from another simulation's formation coefficients. `get_sims()` also keeps the formation coefficients in `nwparam` in step with the first simulation returned, so that `get_nwparam()` agrees with `coef.form`.
-
--   `get_sims()` now returns each requested simulation once, in ascending order as before (a repeated value in `sims` used to error or leave `control$nsims` wrong), and stops with a clear error when `sims` holds a value that is not a whole number of at least 1 (such as `0` or `1.5`).
-
--   Fix `make_restart_point()` to re-base `infTime` by default, as `entrTime` and `exitTime` were; unless listed in `time_attrs`, it was left in the old time frame, so parameters read by duration of infection treated every carried-over infection as new. Also fix it to re-base the unique IDs in the `sus` and `inf` columns of the transmission matrix like those of the attributes, to keep the unique IDs integer, and to name the right attributes in the error raised when a `time_attrs` element is missing.
-
--   Fix `truncate_sim()` for `netsim` objects with `reset.time = TRUE` to shift the times of the attribute history, raw records, transmission matrix and cumulative edgelist to the new time steps, as for the epi trackers, so that `get_attr_at()`, `get_transmat()` and `make_networkDynamic()` read them in the right frame. Records before `at` are kept, with times of 0 or less.
+-   Fix `merge.netsim()` for sequential merges (`Reduce(merge, ...)`) with `keep.*` arguments set to `FALSE`, and to bind epi trackers by name, bind `coef.form` per simulation, and keep the `transmat` class.
 
--   Fix `truncate_sim()` for `netsim` objects to truncate the network statistics with the epi trackers. It kept every time step, so `get_nwstats()` failed on the truncated object with "replacement has N rows", and `make_restart_point()` kept a row from the middle of the run as the last one. `make_restart_point()` now stops on a truncated object, whose `run` state ends after its epi trackers and so re-based the time attributes by the wrong offset; make the restart point before truncating.
+-   `merge.netsim()` no longer fails with "x and y have different controls" for batches read back from disk or run with different `ncores`, `verbose` or checkpoint settings.
 
--   Fix the time steps of a restarted `netsim()` simulation in `as.data.frame()`, `plot()`, `summary()` and `comp_plot()`. Its epi trackers hold every step from 1 to `nsteps`, but since 2.6.0 these methods labeled them from `control$start`, `start - 1` steps too late, so `summary(at = )` and `comp_plot(at = )` reported another step than the one asked for. The steps are now labeled 1 to `nsteps`, in line with the network statistics, transmission matrix and cumulative edgelist. `truncate_sim(reset.time = FALSE)` now stores the label of the first row it keeps in `control$epi.start`, from which these methods, `get_nwstats()` and the formation plot label the steps, rather than in `control$start`, which is now 1 in both cases. Its `at`, like that of `get_attr_at()`, is read on these labels, so an object truncated without reset can be truncated again, and an `at` outside the time steps of the object is now an error. Objects truncated without reset by EpiModel 2.6.x are labeled from 1. Closes #1085.
+-   Fix `get_sims()`, and so `make_restart_point()`, to subset `coef.form`, `attr.history`, `raw.records` and `cumulative.edgelist`, to return a repeated simulation once, and to reject invalid `sims`.
 
--   Fix `netsim()` restarting with `save.nwstats = TRUE` from an object without network statistics (such as one run with `save.nwstats = FALSE`), which failed with "subscript out of bounds". The network statistics of the earlier steps, and of the steps after `end.horizon` removes `summary_nets`, are now `NA`, so they stay aligned with the epi trackers.
+-   Fix `make_restart_point()` to re-base `infTime` and the unique IDs in the transmission matrix.
 
--   Fix the "Setting row names on a tibble is deprecated" warning when `netsim()` restarted from a simulation without transmissions. The empty transmission matrix of `x` is no longer carried over, so the restarted simulation's transmission matrix is a data frame rather than a tibble.
+-   Fix `truncate_sim()` for `netsim` objects to truncate the network statistics and, with `reset.time = TRUE`, to shift the times of the attribute history, raw records, transmission matrix and cumulative edgelist. `make_restart_point()` now stops on a truncated object, so make the restart point first.
 
--   Fix `netsim()` to name the per-simulation `raw.records` (`sim1`, `sim2`, ...) like the other per-simulation elements.
+-   Fix the time steps of a restarted simulation in `as.data.frame()`, `plot()`, `summary()` and `comp_plot()`, which were labeled from `control$start` rather than 1. `truncate_sim(reset.time = FALSE)` now stores the first step's label in `control$epi.start`. Closes #1085.
 
--   Fix `get_attr_history()` for a `netsim` object with a simulation that recorded nothing, which failed with "attempt to apply non-function".
+-   Fix `netsim()` restarts with `save.nwstats = TRUE` from an object without network statistics, and a tibble row names warning on restart from a simulation without transmissions.
 
--   Fix the attribution of transmissions to layers in multi-layer models (`infection.net()` and `infection.2g.net()`). A node infected on more than one layer in the same step was always credited to the lowest-indexed layer, since the discordant edgelists were bound in layer order and the transmission matrix keeps the first successful exposure. The bound edgelist is now shuffled when there is more than one layer, so the recorded infector is a uniform draw among the successful exposures. Single-layer models and the distribution of infections per step are unaffected, but seeded multi-layer runs do not reproduce the results of 2.6.x.
+-   Fix `netsim()` to name `raw.records` by simulation, and `get_attr_history()` for a simulation that recorded nothing.
 
--   Fix the two-group modules for parameters that vary with the duration of infection. `infection.2g.net()` read `inf.prob.g2` by the length of `inf.prob`, so its values were cut short or became `NA`, and `recovery.2g.net()` used only the first value of `rec.rate.g2` when `rec.rate` was a single value. Two-group models with such parameters give different results from 2.6.x.
+-   Fix the layer credited with a transmission in multi-layer models, which was always the lowest-indexed layer when a node was infected on several layers in one step. Seeded multi-layer runs do not reproduce 2.6.x.
 
--   Fix `auto_update_attr()` to accept a fixed `attr.rules` value of `NA`, which made its `if` condition fail.
